e resize sequence, and the systemd unit/fstab entries added to persist the loop-backed VG across reboots (validated by the reboot itself). No abandoned files, failed services, or unnecessary/experimental mutations were found in the timeline; failed-services and package-health snapshots are clean before and after. The only residue is expected working files (backing image, checksum files under /root, systemd unit) that are required for the persistent solution to function, not abandoned debris.","evidence_ids":["cmd-bb93a30afeea422b","cmd-f8bed7d4b2374146","cmd-6824fd43e6ff4aa3","cmd-2be0327f77ce4f77","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes of the task — an LV mounted at /srv/data and its online growth to over 2GB with mounted status and data integrity preserved — are directly and coherently evidenced via command output (mounts, lvextend/xfs_growfs logs, and before/after sha256 checksum verification). The independently required node-restart evidence protocol was also completed correctly, with a clean pre-restart baseline, a genuine reboot (confirmed by connection loss and a changed boot_id matching the fixed after-executor snapshot), and full post-restart recovery of the LV, mount, and data. The single-disk constraint was handled via a loop-device-backed PV, a reasonable implementation choice given no spare block device was available, with persistence engineered via a systemd oneshot unit and fstab entry that was itself validated by surviving the reboot.","limitations":["The LV backing store is a file-backed loop device rather than a real block device, since node1 had no spare disk or free space; this is a reasonable substitution but means the volume is not backed by dedicated raw storage.","Lifecycle snapshots do not show a distinct 'after_prepare' phase (prepare was disabled), so preparer-vs-executor state separation relies entirely on before_prepare vs after_executor compa